<main class="guide wrap" id="main">
  <header class="docs-header">
    <h1>Create H5P content with ChatGPT, Claude or Grok</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An AI chat can write a whole H5P activity, not only its questions: a quiz, flash cards, a
      course presentation or any of <a href="#types">{{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types</a>. Here
      is how, from installing the skill to the <code>.h5p</code> file in your Moodle.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chat needs the h5p-author skill, the instructions that tell it how to write H5P: you
      <a href="#install">install it once</a>. Then the chat writes the activity. Claude and Grok
      make it a <code>.h5p</code> file for Moodle or any other H5P site in the chat, and for
      ChatGPT <a href="/h5p/build/">the build page</a> makes the file in your browser. The skill
      and the page are free and <a href="#open-source">open source</a>, with no account.
    </p>
    <p><a class="button button-solid button-large" href="/h5p/build/">Open the build page</a></p>
  </header>

  {#- The steps are drawn as the skill page's timeline (site.css). The first is the install: the
      zips are written by vite.config.ts from the release the site pins, and each was uploaded as
      it is, by the owner, on 2026-11-13, Claude's into Grok too. Its heading keeps the id that
      the top bar and the skill's page link to. -#}
  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">1</span></span> Install the skill, once</h2>
    <p>
      The skill then stays in your chat, for every activity you ask for. Where it goes depends on
      the chat:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude</strong>, on claude.ai or in its apps: download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author.zip" download>h5p-author.zip</a> and upload it under Customize,
        then Skills, with code execution on. Claude builds the <code>.h5p</code> file in the chat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ChatGPT</strong>: download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author-chatgpt.zip" download>h5p-author-chatgpt.zip</a> and upload it
        under Plugins. ChatGPT's code cannot download the build tool, so download
        <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a> once too,
        and attach it to the message where you ask: ChatGPT then builds the <code>.h5p</code> file in
        the chat. Without it, ChatGPT hands over a link to the build page; if the page says the link
        arrived damaged, ask for the source as a file and open that on the build page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Grok</strong>: download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author.zip" download>h5p-author.zip</a>, the same file as for
        Claude, and upload it under Skills, then Import. Grok builds the <code>.h5p</code> file in
        the chat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open WebUI</strong>: an administrator imports the skill under Import from URL, from
        its folder on GitHub,
        <code>https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/tree/main/skills/h5p-author</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An agent with a terminal</strong>, such as Claude Code, Codex or Cursor:
        <code>npx skills add missing-elements/h5p-offline-player --skill h5p-author</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The zips hold h5p-author {{ skill.version }}, the release the build page runs: text files and
      nothing that runs, which you can read
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/tree/main/skills/h5p-author">on GitHub</a>.
      The archive holds the same release's build tool, which runs in the chat's own sandbox, and
      the H5P libraries it puts in the file, each under its own licence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="ask-heading">
    <h2 id="ask-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">2</span></span> Ask your chat</h2>
    <p>Open a new chat and paste this prompt with your own subject:</p>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P activity on [your subject] for [your students].</pre>
    <p>
      Name the type if you know it, such as "an H5P Question Set of ten questions": the
      <a href="#types">types</a> and <a href="#prompts">prompts to start from</a> are below. For
      another language, say so, such as "in French": the activity's buttons come in it too, where
      H5P has a translation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
    <h2 id="answer-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">3</span></span> Answer its questions</h2>
    <p>
      If the prompt names no type, the chat proposes one or two that fit your material, and you
      choose. Then it asks whose name and which licence go on the activity, which you may skip, and
      writes it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="file-heading">
    <h2 id="file-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">4</span></span> Get the .h5p file</h2>
    <p>
      The build tool is a Node.js program, so what the chat hands you depends on whether its code
      runs Node.js:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the <code>.h5p</code> file itself, from a chat whose code runs Node.js and can reach the build tool, as Claude's and Grok's can, and ChatGPT's with the build archive attached;</li>
      <li>a link that opens the activity on the build page, as ChatGPT gives without it;</li>
      <li>or its source, as a file or a block of JSON: open or paste it <a href="/h5p/build/#build-it">on the build page</a> and choose Build.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      From a link or a source, the build page builds it in your browser, plays it, and saves the
      file with Download the .h5p.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="check-heading">
    <h2 id="check-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">5</span></span> Play it and check it</h2>
    <p>
      Work through all of it, wrong answers included, and check the answers and feedback against
      your material: the build checks the structure, not whether the content is right.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the build lists problems, choose Copy the problems for the chat and paste them into the
      chat that wrote the source. It fixes them all and writes the source again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="course-heading">
    <h2 id="course-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">6</span></span> Add it to your course</h2>
    <p>
      In Moodle, switch Edit mode on in your course, choose Add an activity or resource, then H5P,
      and upload the file under Package file. The file carries the H5P libraries it needs, but by
      default Moodle installs those it lacks only for a manager or an administrator: if it says a
      library is missing, see <a href="#moodle-libraries">below</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Any other H5P site takes it where it adds H5P content, H5P.com too, and Lumi opens it on your
      computer. With no H5P site, h5p-offline-player plays it
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/blob/main/docs/setup.md">on a site of your own</a>
      or <a href="https://h5p-offline-player.vercel.app/app/">offline, in its app</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prompts">
    <h2 id="prompts">Prompts to start from</h2>
    <p>
      Each names its type, so the chat asks only whose name and licence go on it. Change the subject
      and the numbers to yours.
    </p>

    <h3>A course presentation</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the course presentation prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Course Presentation of six slides on writing learning objectives with Bloom's taxonomy, with a question on each slide.</pre>
    <p>
      Claude Haiku made the sample on the build page from this request:
      <a href="/h5p/build/">play it there</a>, or <a href="/h5p/build/sample.h5p" download>download it</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>A quiz</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the quiz prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Question Set of ten multiple-choice questions on the water cycle for 11-year-olds, with feedback that explains each answer.</pre>

    <h3>Flash cards</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the flash cards prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make H5P Dialog Cards for twenty common Spanish verbs: the verb on the front, and its meaning and an example sentence on the back.</pre>

    <h3>Fill in the blanks, from your text</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the fill in the blanks prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Fill in the Blanks from the text below, with its key terms as the blanks.

[Paste your text here]</pre>

    <h3>An interactive book, from your notes</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the interactive book prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Interactive Book from my lesson notes below: a chapter for each topic, each ending with two questions.

[Paste your notes here]</pre>

    <h3>A branching scenario</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the branching scenario prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Branching Scenario in which a new team lead deals with a project running late: three decisions, each with a better and a worse choice, and feedback at every end.</pre>
  </section>

  {#- The types the skill's table offers, in the release the site runs, by their names in H5P's
      editor (src/_data/skill.js): those the chat writes whole, then those that play the user's
      media. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="types">
    <h2 id="types">The {{ skill.offered.length }} H5P content types it offers</h2>
    <p>
      The chat offers these, from the h5p-author skill's list. Name one in your prompt, or say what
      you want and let it propose one.
    </p>

    <h3>From text alone</h3>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | rejectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}.</dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>

    <h3>With your pictures, video or recordings</h3>
    <p>
      Attach your files to the chat, or tell it their names, and it writes the activity around
      them. The build page then asks you to choose them from your computer.
    </p>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | selectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}. <span class="type-media">You add {{ type.media }}.</span></dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
  </section>

  {#- What most people ask first, which the header answers in a word and this section in full. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="open-source">
    <h2 id="open-source">Free and open source</h2>
    <p>
      The build page and the h5p-author skill are free, need no account, and are open source under
      the MIT licence. The build page's code is in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/missing-elements.github.io/tree/main/src/h5p/build">this site's repository</a>,
      and the skill and the player are in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player">h5p-offline-player</a>. The H5P
      runtime the player loads is <a href="/h5p/assets/runtime-LICENSE.txt">GPL-3.0</a>, and the H5P
      libraries in a file are each under their own licence, most MIT, as
      <a href="/h5p/assets/libraries-LICENSES.txt">the pack's licence list</a> says.
    </p>
    <p>
      Privacy first: nothing you make is sent to this site, and there is no MCP server or other
      server of ours in between. The chat builds the file in its own sandbox, or the build page
      builds and plays it in your browser. Your AI chat sees what you send it, as it sees
      anything you ask it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="local">
    <h2 id="local">With a local model, or offline</h2>
    <p>
      A chat app for local models writes the activity as any chat does, but out of the box its code
      does not run Node.js:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Open WebUI's code tool runs Python;</li>
      <li>LM Studio's code plugin runs JavaScript in Deno, not Node.js;</li>
      <li>Jan has no code tool of its own.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      So there the chat hands over a link or the source, and the build page builds the file. Open
      WebUI with Open Terminal is the exception: its default image has Node.js, which the build
      needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      With Node.js on your own computer, the whole build can stay offline. Install the skill in an
      agent with a terminal that runs a local model, from Ollama or LM Studio, and download
      <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a> once.
      Then tell the agent where the archive is. It builds the <code>.h5p</code> file on your
      computer, with no internet and nothing sent anywhere.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="help">
    <h2 id="help">If something goes wrong</h2>

    <h3>The chat doesn't use the skill</h3>
    <p>
      Name it in your message, as the prompts here do: "Use the h5p-author skill to…". If the chat
      knows no such skill, check that it is still installed where <a href="#install">step 1</a> put
      it, and ask in a new chat.
    </p>

    <h3>The chat says it cannot get the build tool</h3>
    <p>
      Download <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a>
      and attach it to your next message: a chat whose code runs Node.js builds the file from it,
      without installing or downloading anything. A chat whose code runs no Node.js gives you a
      link to the build page instead.
    </p>

    <h3>The build lists problems</h3>
    <p>
      Choose Copy the problems for the chat, and paste them into the chat that wrote the source. It
      fixes them all and writes the source again: paste that in place of the old one, and build
      again.
    </p>

    <h3>The chat's link builds nothing</h3>
    <p>
      Ask the chat for the source as JSON instead, and paste it on the build page. A link is made by
      running code, and one a chat wrote out itself breaks.
    </p>

    <h3 id="moodle-libraries">Moodle says a library is missing</h3>
    <p>
      The file carries every library it needs, but by default Moodle lets only managers and
      administrators install new ones. Ask one to upload the file in Site administration, under
      Manage H5P content types: after that, files with the same types work for you too.
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

